{{also|Refrain}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɹeɪn/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-refrain.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-|*dʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|refreynen}}, from {{der|en|xno|refraindre}}, {{der|en|frm|refreindre}} (from {{der|en|la|refringere}}), and {{der|en|xno|refrener}}, {{der|en|frm|refrener}} (from {{der|en|la|refrenare}}).

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[hold]] back, to [[restrain]] (someone or something). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Proverbs|chapter=1|verse=15|passage='''Refrain''' thy foot from their path.}}
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses|14|passage= The presence even for a moment among a party of debauchees of a woman endued with every quality of modesty and not less severe than beautiful '''refrained''' the humorous sallies even of the most licentious but her departure was the signal for an outbreak of ribaldry.}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|archaic}} To show [[restraint]]; to hold oneself back. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1526''', {{w|William Tyndale}}, trans. ''Bible'', Acts V:
#*: And nowe I saye unto you: '''refrayne''' youreselves from these men, and let them alone [...].
#* '''1899''', {{w|Sabine Baring-Gould}}, ''{{w|A Book of the West}}'' [[s:A Book of the West/Volume 1/18|Volume 1 Chapter 18]]
#*:As I went down the river, all dissatisfaction at my lot passed away, and by the time Dartmouth came in view I could no longer '''refrain myself''', but threw my cap into the air, and barely caught it from falling overboard as I shouted, "Hurrah for merry England!{{...}}"
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|_|rare}} To [[repress]] (a desire, emotion etc.); to [[check]] or [[curb]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Chaucer Canterbury|Parson's Tale}}
#*: his reson aperceyueth it wel that it is synne / agayns the lawe of god / and yet his reson / '''refreyneth''' nat his foul delit or talent.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|with preposition "from"}} To stop oneself {{m|en|from}} some [[action]] or [[interference]]; to [[abstain]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Acts|chapter=5|verse=38|passage='''Refrain''' from these men, and let them alone.}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|passage=They '''refrained''' therefrom [eating flesh] some time after.}}
#* '''July 5, 1731''', {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, ''[[s:The Works of the Rev. Jonathan Swift/Volume 12/From Jonathan Swift to Esther Vanhomrigh - 10|letter to Vanessa]]''
#*:If you knew how I struggle for a little health, what uneasiness I am at in riding and walking, and '''refraining''' from every thing agreeable to my taste
#* {{RQ:Wells Time Machine|chapter=The Time Traveler Returns|page=36|passage=I will [...] tell you the story of what has happened to me, if you like, but you must '''refrain''' from interruptions.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare|regional}} To [[abstain]] from (food or drink). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|passage=Who, requiring a remedy for his gout, received no other counsel than to '''refrain''' cold drink.}}

